types (",[18,1613,923],{},[18,1615,626],{},[1583,1617,1618,1619,1621],{},"Reducing ",[18,1620,20],{}," + type assertions.",[14,1623,1624,517],{},[1576,1625,1626],{},"Avoid generics for",[1580,1628,1629,1632,1635],{},[1583,1630,1631],{},"Single-use code (just write the concrete version).",[1583,1633,1634],{},"When an interface is clearer (small interfaces compose well).",[1583,1636,1637,1638,1640],{},"Over-abstraction (a generic ",[18,1639,1600],{}," is fine; a generic framework is often over-engineered).",[23,1642,1644],{"id":1643},"tips-tricks","💡 Tips & Tricks",[1580,1646,1647,1673,1691,1726,1734],{},[1583,1648,1649,1652,1653,1208,1655,1657,1658,62,1660,62,1662,62,1664,62,1666,62,1668,62,1670,1672],{},[1576,1650,1651],{},"Idiom",": prefer the ",[18,1654,1207],{},[18,1656,1211],{}," packages (Go 1.21+) over hand-rolled generic helpers — they cover the common operations (",[18,1659,1298],{},[18,1661,1311],{},[18,1663,1358],{},[18,1665,1335],{},[18,1667,1415],{},[18,1669,1433],{},[18,1671,1457],{},") and are tested and idiomatic. Reach for your own generics only when the stdlib doesn't cover the case.",[1583,1674,1675,1677,1678,1681,1682,397,1685,1687,1688,558],{},[1576,1676,1651],{},": use ",[18,1679,1680],{},"cmp.Ordered"," (Go 1.21+) for \"any orderable type\" — it's the standard constraint for ",[18,1683,1684],{},"\u003C",[18,1686,591],{}," comparisons, covering integers, floats, and strings. Pre-1.21, define the constraint or use ",[18,1689,1690],{},"golang.org\u002Fx\u002Fexp\u002Fconstraints",[1583,1692,1693,1677,1695,1698,1699,1702,1703,1705,1706,1709,1710,1713,1714,1716,1717,1720,1721,1723,1724,558],{},[1576,1694,1651],{},[18,1696,1697],{},"~T"," in constraints to include named types — ",[18,1700,1701],{},"~int"," matches ",[18,1704,205],{}," and any ",[18,1707,1708],{},"type MyInt int",", so ",[18,1711,1712],{},"type UserID int"," still satisfies ",[18,1715,1701],{}," constraints. Without ",[18,1718,1719],{},"~",", a named type based on ",[18,1722,205],{}," wouldn't match ",[18,1725,205],{},[1583,1727,1728,1730,1731,1733],{},[1576,1729,1651],{},": don't over-genericize — if a function is used with one type, write it concretely. Generics shine for reusable containers and algorithms; over-abstracting single-use code adds complexity without benefit. \"Don't write a generic ",[18,1732,1600],{}," if you only have one map call.\"",[1583,1735,1736,1738,1739,1741,1742,1744,1745,1747],{},[1576,1737,1651],{},": use generics to replace ",[18,1740,20],{}," + type assertions where the type is known at the call site — a ",[18,1743,1588],{}," is type-safe (no ",[18,1746,334],{},", no assertion), and the compiler catches type mismatches at the call, not at runtime.",[23,1749,1751],{"id":1750},"️-edge-cases-gotchas","⚠️ Edge Cases & Gotchas",[1580,1753,1754,1760,1766,1775,1808,1814,1826,1844],{},[1583,1755,1756,1759],{},[1576,1757,1758],{},"Generics don't work with methods",": Go doesn't support generic methods (methods with their own type parameters, distinct from the receiver's). Methods can use the receiver's type parameters, but can't add new ones. This is a known limitation.",[1583,1761,1762,1765],{},[1576,1763,1764],{},"Type inference can fail",": when the inference is ambiguous (multiple possible type arguments), specify explicitly. The compiler error is usually clear.",[1583,1767,1768,1774],{},[1576,1769,1770,1773],{},[18,1771,1772],{},"comparable"," includes interfaces",": interfaces are comparable (with the nil-interface caveat — comparing interfaces holding slices\u002Fmaps panics at runtime).",[1583,1776,1777,1390,1782,1702,1784,1208,1786,1788,1789,1791,1792,1794,1795,1797,1798,1801,1802,1804,1805,1807],{},[1576,1778,1779,1781],{},[18,1780,1697],{}," and named types",[18,1783,1701],{},[18,1785,205],{},[18,1787,1708],{},", but ",[18,1790,205],{}," (without ",[18,1793,1719],{},") matches only the predeclared ",[18,1796,205],{},", not ",[18,1799,1800],{},"MyInt",". Always use ",[18,1803,1719],{}," if you want named types based on ",[18,1806,59],{}," to satisfy the constraint.",[1583,1809,1810,1813],{},[1576,1811,1812],{},"No specialization",": you can't provide a special implementation for a specific type argument (like C++ template specialization). Write separate functions if you need type-specific behavior.",[1583,1815,1816,1390,1819,1822,1823,1825],{},[1576,1817,1818],{},"Constraint unions can be large",[18,1820,1821],{},"int | int8 | int16 | ..."," is verbose. Use the standard constraints (",[18,1824,1680],{},") or factor common unions into a named constraint interface.",[1583,1827,1828,1390,1831,1834,1835,1837,1838,1841,1842,1201],{},[1576,1829,1830],{},"Generic types can have methods, but not generic methods",[18,1832,1833],{},"func (s *Stack[T]) Push(v T)"," is fine (uses ",[18,1836,59],{},"); ",[18,1839,1840],{},"func (s *Stack[T]) Map[U any](fn func(T) U) []U"," is illegal (adds ",[18,1843,65],{},[1583,1845,1846,1849],{},[1576,1847,1848],{},"Instantiation cost",": each distinct type argument set creates a new instantiation (monomorphization). Many distinct types can increase binary size, though the compiler deduplicates where possible.",[23,1851,1853],{"id":1852},"spot-the-bug","🧠 Spot the Bug",[14,1855,1856,1857,1859],{},"A developer writes a generic ",[18,1858,923],{}," but it doesn't compile:",[28,1861,1862],{"language":30},[32,1863,1865],{"className":34,"code":1864,"language":30,"meta":36,"style":36},"func Sum[T Number](s []T) T {\n    var total T\n    for _, v := range s {\n        total += v\n    }\n    return total\n}\n\ntype Number interface {\n    int | float64\n}\n\ntype Score int\n\nfunc main() {\n    scores := []Score{10, 20, 30}\n    fmt.Println(Sum(scores))   \u002F\u002F ERROR: Score doesn't satisfy Number\n}\n",[18,1866,1867,1893,1901,1913,1921,1925,1931,1935,1939,1949,1957,1961,1965,1975,1979,1989,2018,2036],{"__ignoreMap":36},[40,1868,1869,1871,1873,1875,1877,1879,1881,1883,1885,1887,1889,1891],{"class":42,"line":43},[40,1870,47],{"class":46},[40,1872,844],{"c